# Resonance notes: (anchor, lemma-akkadian, html). All rendered in the outer margin with the ≈ register.
RES = {
 'I': [
  ('47','Gilgāmeš … nabi šumšu','“Gilgameš was his name from the day of his birth”: the poem opens its hero with an act of naming. The name is Sumerian Bilgames — by the old folk-reading, “the forefather is a hero” — worn smooth into Akkadian. In this poem to be named is the surviving form of being: the creed of II 232–240, the wager of the OB Yale tablet, and the final wall all traffic in <i>šumu</i>, “name.”'),
  ('100','ṭīda iktariṣ — the clay ring','Aruru pinches off <i>ṭiṭṭu</i>, clay, and Enkidu exists. The word closes every circle the poem opens: Enkidu “turned to clay” (X 68), the drowned world “gone back to clay” (XI 135), Ištar wishing the fatal day itself “had turned back to clay” (XI 119). Creation and annihilation are one substance; English “clay” keeps the word but not the ring.'),
  ('102','kiṣir Ninurta ≈ kiṣru ša Ānu','Enkidu is <i>kiṣir Ninurta</i>, a “knot” of Ninurta’s strength; the dream-meteor of line 246 is <i>kiṣru ša Ānu</i>, a “lump” of Anu. One noun, <i>kiṣru</i> — knot, clot, concentration — binds the omen to the man before any interpreter speaks: the dream is solved lexically. See the note at 246.'),
  ('176','Šamhat ≈ šamhu','Her name is the common adjective <i>šamhu</i>, “lush, luxuriant, magnificent” — a professional name that is also Uruk’s own epithet-family. In the OB Pennsylvania tablet the dreaming king says <i>šamhākū-ma</i>, “I walked in my luxuriance” (OB II 4–5): he struts in her name a column before he hears it. The English “Šamhat” is mute; the Akkadian ear hears “Lady Lush.”'),
  ('36','Rīmat-Ninsun — the pastoral lattice','Ninsun’s Sumerian name means “Lady Wild-Cow,” which the Akkadian doubles as the epithet <i>rīmatu</i>. Around her the poem builds a onomastic pasture: the city is <i>supūru</i>, “the Sheepfold”; the king is <i>rīmu</i>, “wild bull”; his people, in the complaint of 71–72, are the cows. The metaphors are not ornament — they are the names.'),
  ('246','kiṣru ša Ānu ≈ kiṣir Ninurta','The falling “lump of Anu” repeats the noun that made Enkidu at 102 — and the fixed simile of his strength (“hard as a lump of the sky-rock of Anu,” 123, 135, 150 …) keeps the equation sounding through the whole tablet. The friend is the meteor; the omen was never obscure.'),
 ],
 'II': [
  ('222','napīssu mūtu — breath and life','Humbaba’s “breath” is <i>napīšu</i>, blowing; “life” throughout the poem is <i>napištu</i>, the throat-breath — one root, n-p-š. His breath is death; the plant of XI 295–296 lets a man “reach again the breath of his life” (<i>napšassu</i>). The monster and the medicine are declensions of the same lung.'),
  ('235','šārum-ma ≈ šār','“Whatever he ever does is only wind” — <i>šāru</i>, wind. The prologue and the finale measure Uruk in <i>šār</i>, the unit of 3,600 (I 22–23; XI 327). The two words are unrelated and sound alike, and the poem ends inside the pun: what a man does is <i>šār</i> — wind, or a city’s measure, depending on which he builds. See the wall at XI 323–328.'),
 ],
 'VI': [
  ('9','mutu ≈ mūtu — husband and death','“You be my husband (<i>mutu</i>)…” — a short vowel from <i>mūtu</i>, “death,” the poem’s obsessive noun. The catalogue of lovers that follows is the pun worked out in six case-studies: to be Ištar’s <i>mutu</i> is to meet <i>mūtu</i>. Akkadian ears did not need the lovers listed to hear the danger in the proposal.'),
 ],
 'X': [
  ('87','Ur-šanabi — servant of Two-Thirds','The boatman’s name parses as “Servant-of-Šanabi,” and <i>šanabi</i> is the sexagesimal word for 40 — which written as a fraction is two-thirds, and as a divine number is Ea’s. The ferryman of the Waters of Death is thus “servant of two-thirds”: piloting the hero who is “two-thirds god” (I 48, IX 51) on Ea’s own element. The OB form Sursunabu wears the same cipher more thickly.'),
  ('208','Ūta-napišti ≈ tuttâ napišta','The name means “He-Found-Life” (<i>ūta</i> “he found” + <i>napišti</i>): the goal of the quest is a man whose name is its answer in the past tense. At XI 207–208 the survivor closes his tale on the same verb — “who will gather the gods for you, that you may <i>find</i> (<i>tuttâ</i>) the life you are hunting?” — sentencing Gilgameš with the etymology of his own name. Untranslatable, and the hinge of the poem.'),
 ],
 'XI': [
  ('14','abūbu — the word before the thing','“The great gods’ heart moved them to make the Flood”: <i>abūbu</i>. The word has been sounding since Tablet II — Humbaba’s voice “is the Flood” (II 221, V), the whip-crack boast of V 135, the wave-epithet of the king himself (I 34). The poem spends ten tablets using the Flood as a simile for power; here the simile stands up and drowns the world.'),
  ('49','Atra-hasīs — the older name','Mid-narrative the survivor is suddenly “Atra-hasīs,” “Surpassing-Wise” — the hero’s name in the older Babylonian Flood epic, left standing in the masonry like a reused brick stamped by the previous king. The seam is the citation.'),
  ('196','Ea’s defense ≈ Ea’s trick','“I did not open the secret — I let him see a dream”: Ea repeats to Enlil’s face the reed-fence evasion of lines 20–22. The two passages rhyme legally: an oath kept to the letter twice, and the letter is the loophole.'),
 ],
 'XII': [
  ('52','erṣetu — earth and underworld','“The earth seized him”: <i>erṣetu</i> is at once soil, ground, territory, and the netherworld itself — the refrain of this tablet is a pun the English cannot keep. The ball fell “into the earth”; Enkidu went down “to the earth”; the earth that seizes is the same word a farmer ploughs. The dead are not elsewhere; they are underfoot.'),
 ],
}

